Deploying a small web application behind a reverse proxy is mostly a matter of writing the pieces down once and then keeping them in step. This walkthrough assumes a fresh Debian server, a non-root account with sudo, and a domain name whose DNS already points at the machine.

Install the proxy and the certificate tooling, disable the default site so that only your own configuration is served, and create a system user for the application. The file that matters is the virtual host definition under /etc/nginx/sites-available. It should set the server name, forward requests to the application's Unix socket and add the usual forwarding headers so the application records the client address rather than the proxy address.

server {
    listen 80;
    server_name app.example.org;

    location / {
        proxy_pass http://unix:/run/app/app.sock;
        proxy_set_header Host $host;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $remote_addr;
    }
}

Link the file into sites-enabled, run nginx -t to check the syntax and reload the service instead of restarting it, so that open connections are not dropped. Then request a certificate for the domain, confirm that the renewal timer is enabled, and block plain HTTP once the redirect works.

The application itself runs under a systemd unit with Restart=always, a dedicated User, WorkingDirectory=/srv/app and an EnvironmentFile at /etc/app/env. Keep secrets out of the unit file; anything in the unit is visible to every local user through systemctl show. Follow the journal with journalctl -u app -f while you test, and remember that the unit must be reloaded and the daemon restarted after you edit it.

The most common failure at this stage is a 502 Bad Gateway. Check that the socket exists, that its group is www-data and that the proxy can traverse /run/app. A quick ls -l on the socket and a systemctl status on the unit will usually show whether the application is listening at all or whether the proxy is simply pointed at the wrong path.

Open only the ports you need: 80 and 443 for the proxy, 22 for administration, and leave the application socket unreachable from outside. A short firewall rule set is easier to audit than a long one, and it is worth writing down which service owns each open port.

Log rotation deserves a moment as well. The proxy and the application both write to the journal, but if you also keep access logs in /var/log/nginx, make sure logrotate is configured to compress and expire them, or the disk will fill quietly and the first symptom will be a failed deploy.

Finally, arrange backups before you need them. A nightly rsync into a separate volume plus a weekly snapshot is enough for a small service, and restoring one file from a snapshot is far easier than reconstructing a database from a partial dump. Test the restore once; an untested backup is a rumour.

